750
00:57:33,049 --> 00:57:39,035
[SPEAKER_21]: I'm Timothy Fraser, I'm an attorney at Dane Torpy 175 Federal Street, Boston.

751
00:57:40,656 --> 00:57:45,481
[SPEAKER_21]: And I guess what I want to do now is kind of talk to the zoning variances that are required for the project.

752
00:57:45,741 --> 00:58:02,847
[SPEAKER_21]: which, as of now are essentially three, and we ended up these three with these three variances based on the extensive collaboration that Quinlan had mentioned throughout the process with the C offices, particularly the planning and community development boards as part of the site plan review for the project.

753
00:58:04,005 --> 00:58:11,750
[SPEAKER_21]: When we began the project and we first presented it, the design triggered a number of zoning violations, and those were particularly related to the setback requirements.

754
00:58:12,691 --> 00:58:27,500
[SPEAKER_21]: Because of the narrow shape of the lot, which we'll see in a second, and there's also a chapter 91 waterway restricted portion that's facing the street side, we previously had side yard violations on both sides and a rear yard setback violation.

755
00:58:28,320 --> 00:58:31,883
[SPEAKER_21]: And you'll see that actually in the description that's on the project agenda.

756
00:58:32,743 --> 00:58:52,516
[SPEAKER_21]: So we were before the board in May 2022 and then we continued the matter and during that time, we continued the discussions that we were having with the planning and the community development boards were able to work through our design to eliminate those setback violations while still providing for the access road and some of the other design features that will also get to.

757
00:58:53,376 --> 00:59:02,693
[SPEAKER_21]: So at this time, we now present to the board a design that triggers three variances as you see there on the slide for lot coverage, loading bays, and height.

758
00:59:03,710 --> 00:59:12,993
[SPEAKER_21]: And particularly, with respect to loading bays the zoning ordinances call for five loading bays are designed, excuse me our design has three loading bays.

759
00:59:13,894 --> 00:59:22,096
[SPEAKER_21]: With respect to lot coverage the ordinances require a 50% use ratio, our project is over by just a half a percent we're at 50.5%.

760
00:59:24,117 --> 00:59:29,440
[SPEAKER_21]: And then with respect to height, we're actually seeking to variances essentially for both height and number of stories.

761
00:59:30,080 --> 00:59:41,827
[SPEAKER_21]: The ordinances set the height limit at 30 feet, we're at 165 feet, and for stories the ordinances set the limit at two stories and we're at eight stories with the mechanicals penthouse.

762
00:59:43,676 --> 00:59:49,921
[SPEAKER_21]: So with respect to those variances we of course analyze the city's zoning ordinances and also the comprehensive plan.

763
00:59:50,962 --> 01:00:01,991
[SPEAKER_21]: So essentially, you know, on one hand you have the currently enacted zoning ordinances which are more suited to low rise manufacturing facilities and warehouses and distribution centers that were envisioned at that time.

764
01:00:02,431 --> 01:00:11,298
[SPEAKER_21]: But on the other hand, you have the comprehensive plan, which is, we think more forward thinking and designed to be that to attract these types of new innovative business

765
01:00:11,538 --> 01:00:13,819
[SPEAKER_21]: like this life science building that we're proposing.

766
01:00:14,719 --> 01:00:33,227
[SPEAKER_21]: But to develop that type of life science building on this site or a site such as this, and particularly a life science company, they require certain design components to ensure that they're functional and that they're expandable and flexible and in line with the modern life science activities.

767
01:00:34,092 --> 01:00:45,177
[SPEAKER_21]: So without these variances being granted, or at least in our case, the property owners would be compelled by the zoning ordinances to make the site available for use that is not really a ties to best use.

768
01:00:45,638 --> 01:00:49,480
[SPEAKER_21]: So that's our hardship for the applicant on this site.

769
01:00:50,440 --> 01:01:00,725
[SPEAKER_21]: And I believe I've provided the board in a letter yesterday, a copy of the Rashi lead case, which stands for that proposition that this site, based

770
01:01:03,273 --> 01:01:05,094
[SPEAKER_21]: the substantial hardship to the developer.

771
01:01:06,655 --> 01:01:19,622
[SPEAKER_21]: But in addition to that highest and best use hardship, the site's narrowness itself and its topography, which we'll also get into, and some of the other characteristics also present challenges to developing the site.

772
01:01:20,362 --> 01:01:25,325
[SPEAKER_21]: And so I think we just have following just a few slides that illustrate those challenges and how

773
01:01:29,905 --> 01:01:32,346
[SPEAKER_21]: slide deck, you can hit the next slide.

774
01:01:32,826 --> 01:01:33,046
[SPEAKER_21]: Great.

775
01:01:34,306 --> 01:01:41,868
[SPEAKER_21]: So here is the site restrictions, it really illustrates some of the difficulties that we encountered with the narrow shape of the lot.

776
01:01:42,228 --> 01:01:49,190
[SPEAKER_21]: And just for reference on the, on the left side of your screen, that's Mystic Valley Parkway it's headed west up towards the top of your screen.

777
01:01:50,290 --> 01:01:58,473
[SPEAKER_21]: So, and I think the most prominent note on this slide, you'll see is that 61 foot chapter 91 waterway restriction setback.

778
01:01:59,533 --> 01:02:03,674
[SPEAKER_21]: I think Quinlan mentioned a long time ago that this site was marshlands.

779
01:02:04,235 --> 01:02:08,216
[SPEAKER_21]: So we sort of carved the design in the front of the building around.

780
01:02:09,076 --> 01:02:28,683
[SPEAKER_21]: That, that chapter 91 setback and it allowed us to fill out the front of the lot, but still incorporate a nice publicly accessible Plaza in a walkable area that's open to the public and we'll have chairs and other gathering items, and it creates a nice urban streetscape along the along the parkway.

781
01:02:29,303 --> 01:02:32,924
[SPEAKER_21]: And then you'll see the red dash that kind of surrounds the building there.

782
01:02:34,045 --> 01:02:49,091
[SPEAKER_21]: That's how the field building footprint was kind of modified as a result of our discussions and collaborations to fit within that 15 foot boundary setback so that we don't have any setback violations anymore, where we still have the access we're going around.

783
01:02:52,700 --> 01:02:52,940
[SPEAKER_21]: Great.

784
01:02:53,480 --> 01:03:09,125
[SPEAKER_21]: This is a slide that you can see here the emergency access road that was required around the building, as well as to the bottom of the screen, the loading bays, and also towards the top right, you see the ramp leading to the underground parking.

785
01:03:10,105 --> 01:03:19,768
[SPEAKER_21]: And again, it was the lot's narrowness that really exacerbated the need for that emergency access road because, you know, without

786
01:03:21,406 --> 01:03:29,414
[SPEAKER_21]: the access road really couldn't be put without triggering a setback, but through our conversations, we were actually able to achieve that.

787
01:03:30,615 --> 01:03:33,838
[SPEAKER_21]: And obviously we considered safety and also traffic flows.

788
01:03:33,858 --> 01:03:37,322
[SPEAKER_21]: So you'll see that traffic flows only in one direction.

789
01:03:38,002 --> 01:03:44,869
[SPEAKER_21]: We maintain the existing curb cuts, and then it leads to the underground parking at the rear of the site.

790
01:03:46,077 --> 01:04:07,776
[SPEAKER_21]: And actually, with respect to that underground parking, it not only provides us with the ability to create the adequate number of parking spaces on the site, but it also required and allows us to actually raise the building, which lets us put the stormwater systems and stormwater maintenance systems underneath the building, which we'll get to in a second.

791
01:04:07,816 --> 01:04:09,937
[SPEAKER_21]: And so that's also contributing to some of the height.

792
01:04:09,958 --> 01:04:12,860
[SPEAKER_21]: You wanna go to the next slide?

793
01:04:14,850 --> 01:04:23,495
[SPEAKER_21]: So here and I don't want to get over my skis with the, with the, the way the stormwater management works but you can see that in our design, you know,

794
01:04:24,462 --> 01:04:34,625
[SPEAKER_21]: not only paid specific attention to the damage to the surrounding topography, but we actually developed a stormwater plan that improves on the current stormwater plan.

795
01:04:35,746 --> 01:04:48,010
[SPEAKER_21]: Currently, right now, as you're familiar with the site, it's a big parking lot, it's an impervious parking lot that flows when it rains, the water flows just right to the wetlands, right next to the

796
01:04:52,880 --> 01:04:54,040
[SPEAKER_21]: vehicle fluids, etc.

797
01:04:54,580 --> 01:05:03,222
[SPEAKER_21]: And our site significantly improves that and we do have members of our team here who worked on the stormwater plan if you have any details technically about how that was achieved.

798
01:05:07,483 --> 01:05:14,505
[SPEAKER_21]: And then the next slide is, these are really here to give an understanding of the design of what a modern lab looks like.

799
01:05:22,743 --> 01:05:33,091
[SPEAKER_21]: And if, I don't know, Brian, do you want to jump in and just kind of give us a little bit of detail on how a lab or a modern day lab would fit on this site?

800
01:05:33,771 --> 01:05:34,031
[SPEAKER_15]: Sure.

801
01:05:34,132 --> 01:05:36,593
[SPEAKER_15]: So thanks everybody and good evening board.

802
01:05:37,434 --> 01:05:38,295
[SPEAKER_15]: My name is Brian Cook.

803
01:05:38,355 --> 01:05:39,976
[SPEAKER_15]: I'm a principal of architecture at Jacobs.

804
01:05:40,456 --> 01:05:48,142
[SPEAKER_15]: So we thought it would be a good opportunity in three or four slides, quick ones, to give it a description of

805
01:05:49,090 --> 01:05:53,013
[SPEAKER_15]: what a lab building is in comparison to an office building.

806
01:05:53,074 --> 01:05:55,636
[SPEAKER_15]: And, you know, we've really simplified it in a perfect world.

807
01:05:55,676 --> 01:06:02,802
[SPEAKER_15]: We'd love these things to be bricks, these perfect little rectangles, because they'd be easy to lay out for all the scientists and all their equipment inside.

808
01:06:02,862 --> 01:06:12,150
[SPEAKER_15]: But as you can see by the constraints of our site, 4060 Mississippi Parkway, that this shape wouldn't be ideal.

809
01:06:12,270 --> 01:06:14,472
[SPEAKER_15]: However, labs work on a module.

810
01:06:15,135 --> 01:06:35,328
[SPEAKER_15]: So what you can see here in the Goldilocks zone, kind of in the middle and to the bottom of the page, the 45 foot lease steps with the core, the building core, where your elevators and stairs are in the center, 45 to 55 feet is kind of the Goldilocks zone for lab modules these days because of the number of benches and the number of different types of equipment they need.

811
01:06:36,249 --> 01:06:43,914
[SPEAKER_15]: In the other direction, Quinn, if you go to the next slide, these buildings work on a very strict,

812
01:06:45,047 --> 01:06:45,688
[SPEAKER_15]: 11 foot module.

813
01:06:46,548 --> 01:06:54,833
[SPEAKER_15]: And the way you get that 11 foot modules, you have two work benches and a five foot aisle between each sets of columns, right?

814
01:06:54,993 --> 01:07:00,917
[SPEAKER_15]: So all of the lab equipment that's made is based on that type of modularity.

815
01:07:01,077 --> 01:07:07,061
[SPEAKER_15]: So as you go into these spaces, if they have to take out an old piece of equipment, put a new piece of equipment, you don't even think about it, you know, it's going to fit.

816
01:07:07,481 --> 01:07:11,023
[SPEAKER_15]: So that's how all lab lab case work works.

817
01:07:11,664 --> 01:07:12,344
[SPEAKER_15]: Next slide, please.

818
01:07:13,570 --> 01:07:19,534
[SPEAKER_15]: So as you get and figure out your plan and how that fits on the site, the next thing is the floor to floor height.

819
01:07:19,594 --> 01:07:23,636
[SPEAKER_15]: So office buildings, we could do 13 foot floor to floor all day long.

820
01:07:23,656 --> 01:07:25,077
[SPEAKER_15]: There's a number of these in Medford.

821
01:07:26,178 --> 01:07:28,559
[SPEAKER_15]: Lab offices really need 15 feet.

822
01:07:28,879 --> 01:07:33,142
[SPEAKER_15]: And that's because the amount of air that we move in and out of these buildings that are required by code.

823
01:07:33,622 --> 01:07:39,505
[SPEAKER_15]: So the interstitial space, what's above that ceiling is incredibly important in lab buildings.

824
01:07:39,565 --> 01:07:41,967
[SPEAKER_15]: And that's why these are taller floor to floor heights.

825
01:07:43,530 --> 01:07:44,731
[SPEAKER_15]: The next slide, please, Quinn.

826
01:07:45,732 --> 01:07:50,415
[SPEAKER_15]: And the other big difference, which I'm sure you've seen, is the penthouse, the mechanical penthouse.

827
01:07:50,435 --> 01:07:55,178
[SPEAKER_15]: So we just talked about all that air that gets moved through, but there's a lot of equipment that goes up into these.

828
01:07:56,059 --> 01:08:03,284
[SPEAKER_15]: A lot of the times, it's about the size of the floor plate of the building itself, and then another two floors, plus or minus, about 40 feet tall.

829
01:08:04,425 --> 01:08:05,806
[SPEAKER_15]: But that was old labs.

830
01:08:06,806 --> 01:08:10,549
[SPEAKER_15]: New labs these days, with all of the high-end

831
01:08:12,183 --> 01:08:22,269
[SPEAKER_15]: energy efficiency and really trying to get carbon reduction, carbon neutrality, and where we're all going as a design community, these are even getting more packed.

832
01:08:23,210 --> 01:08:28,173
[SPEAKER_15]: And as we try to get more efficient, these types of machines get bigger and bigger and bigger.

833
01:08:28,253 --> 01:08:32,615
[SPEAKER_15]: So that's why these lab buildings have such a tall hat.

834
01:08:32,715 --> 01:08:40,060
[SPEAKER_15]: And you'll see in the team when they go through some of these things that we've disguised this in a way to make it blend in and not be so intrusive.

835
01:08:41,506 --> 01:08:43,188
[SPEAKER_15]: Next slide, Quinn, I think it's back to you.

836
01:08:48,412 --> 01:09:02,384
[SPEAKER_21]: I guess before we open up in questions, I'll just say in closing that, or at least for the variance portion of the presentation, we're requesting from the board variances for the loading bay, the lot area ratio and height in both feet and stories.

837
01:09:03,425 --> 01:09:07,529
[SPEAKER_21]: And based on our analysis, we feel that the variances are not detrimental to the public good.

838
01:09:07,689 --> 01:09:08,670
[SPEAKER_21]: In fact, we have

839
01:09:11,373 --> 01:09:17,596
[SPEAKER_21]: good that come along with the project, improvements to the streetscape, providing a public area with outdoor furniture and the like.

840
01:09:17,676 --> 01:09:22,338
[SPEAKER_21]: There'll be a new six-foot sidewalk that runs along the south frontage of the site.

841
01:09:23,239 --> 01:09:26,480
[SPEAKER_21]: And it also improves, as discussed, the stormwater management.

842
01:09:26,520 --> 01:09:29,322
[SPEAKER_21]: That's from a huge improvement from what's currently there.

843
01:09:30,582 --> 01:09:38,146
[SPEAKER_21]: The variances, if they're granted, would still be in harmony with the general purpose of the zoning ordinances and not injurious to the neighborhood.

844
01:09:46,546 --> 01:09:53,254
[SPEAKER_21]: request from the board to grant the relief, and I'm happy to have questions addressed to me or to any of the specific members of the development team.

845
01:09:54,415 --> 01:09:54,776
[SPEAKER_27]: Okay, great.

846
01:09:54,836 --> 01:09:55,937
[SPEAKER_27]: Thank you so much, Attorney Frazier.

847
01:09:56,938 --> 01:09:57,258
[SPEAKER_21]: Thank you.

