970
01:20:41,293 --> 01:20:48,355
[SPEAKER_19]: And I was wondering if you could just speak to how much height the stormwater aspect ended up adding to the building.

971
01:20:50,345 --> 01:20:59,335
[SPEAKER_21]: Thank you, Mr. Caldera, I'll actually pass that back to Quinlan he can feel that to the to the actual design team, as far as actual people or stories.

972
01:20:59,936 --> 01:21:06,122
[SPEAKER_21]: I can tell you in general, that the parking underneath required the building to be raised

973
01:21:14,142 --> 01:21:17,504
[SPEAKER_17]: Yeah, I'm actually, I'm actually gonna, sorry, pass the baton one more time.

974
01:21:17,544 --> 01:21:21,586
[SPEAKER_17]: We do have Mark Jungens, who is the civil engineer for VHB on the call tonight.

975
01:21:22,467 --> 01:21:28,470
[SPEAKER_17]: Mark, I'm gonna go ahead and put this slide up just, I think it'd be helpful for you to talk through the stormwater plan there.

976
01:21:30,491 --> 01:21:32,532
[SPEAKER_16]: Like a lot of things, it's a complicated answer.

977
01:21:33,953 --> 01:21:40,557
[SPEAKER_16]: We have a few things driving the way this building meets the ground, which, you know, sort of sets part of its side, a small portion of it.

978
01:21:42,259 --> 01:21:46,781
[SPEAKER_16]: We started when we looked at this project at the front of the building.

979
01:21:46,961 --> 01:21:52,563
[SPEAKER_16]: We wanted to meet Mystic Valley Parkway as well as it can and be accessible, and that's where our driveways are.

980
01:21:53,264 --> 01:22:02,887
[SPEAKER_16]: But we also knew that in order to create a drainage system for this site that actually does something, because right now there is no drainage system on this existing site.

981
01:22:03,007 --> 01:22:07,469
[SPEAKER_16]: All the trash and oil and sand and everything just flows on into the wetland.

982
01:22:07,569 --> 01:22:09,750
[SPEAKER_16]: There's no pipes or catch basins or anything.

983
01:22:10,480 --> 01:22:21,529
[SPEAKER_16]: So we end when you get to the end of the site, you're a little bit above the wetland, but not enough that you can generate that depth that you need for a drainage system to capture it and control it and treat it.

984
01:22:23,010 --> 01:22:34,139
[SPEAKER_16]: So we also have a different parameter, which you can see on these sections here is the blue line is essentially a 1% 2030 flood line.

985
01:22:34,159 --> 01:22:34,580
[SPEAKER_16]: And so

986
01:22:39,899 --> 01:22:46,783
[SPEAKER_16]: If you start at Mystic Valley Parkway, it's elevation nine and it flows to the back of the site to about elevation four.

987
01:22:47,664 --> 01:22:50,486
[SPEAKER_16]: So this flood elevation is about 1135.

988
01:22:52,207 --> 01:23:09,138
[SPEAKER_16]: So the other thing driving kind of where we set the bottom of the building was coming up from Mystic Valley Parkway high enough that we can get the finished floor as close or above this, that hundred year flood elevation or the 1% flood elevation.

989
01:23:10,076 --> 01:23:28,364
[SPEAKER_16]: quickly and then set the building somewhat level so that if we do have a storm event we can protect the building and that's driving kind of where that first floor is and at the same time that gives us the height that we need and we have to fill in the site around the building if you would.

990
01:23:28,944 --> 01:23:32,826
[SPEAKER_16]: It gives us that depth that we need to install drainage infrastructure and a

991
01:23:33,945 --> 01:23:47,136
[SPEAKER_16]: subsurface detention facility and a subsurface treatment facility so that when this thing is complete, we're going to limit the peak runoff from the site to less than what goes there now.

992
01:23:47,757 --> 01:23:56,985
[SPEAKER_16]: We're going to treat the stormwater and we're going to have all the facilities to control grit, floating oils and gases and trash.

993
01:23:58,098 --> 01:24:07,729
[SPEAKER_16]: And so that kind of brought, if you look at it from Mystic Valley Park where you just see this gentle slope up, which is less than 5% of course, so it's accessible and it's a plaza.

994
01:24:08,951 --> 01:24:15,859
[SPEAKER_16]: But because the site drops away as you move to the back of the site, you see that height that we're using to make all those things happen.

995
01:24:19,932 --> 01:24:28,155
[SPEAKER_19]: Okay, and so Mr. Triggins, I know you said it's complicated, but if I'm understanding you correctly, we're talking like on the order of five to 10 feet.

996
01:24:28,696 --> 01:24:33,518
[SPEAKER_16]: Is that how much the man- I think it's seven or eight feet at the back.

997
01:24:33,638 --> 01:24:34,838
[SPEAKER_16]: Okay.

998
01:24:34,878 --> 01:24:36,779
[SPEAKER_16]: You don't really see much of anything in the front.

999
01:24:38,200 --> 01:24:45,683
[SPEAKER_17]: Yeah, I think it's about, and Mark, correct me if I'm wrong, at the front of the property, it's about a two foot raise, right, from nine to 11 feet.

1000
01:24:47,104 --> 01:24:48,124
[SPEAKER_16]: Yeah, that's correct.

1001
01:24:50,774 --> 01:24:52,955
[SPEAKER_19]: It looks like we have Director Hunt.

1002
01:24:53,615 --> 01:24:53,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1003
01:24:57,197 --> 01:24:59,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Good evening, board members.

1004
01:25:00,559 --> 01:25:08,402
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't mind, I actually thought it would be helpful as part of this question to comment on climate change and sea level rise.

1005
01:25:09,163 --> 01:25:12,424
[SPEAKER_01]: And our zoning doesn't speak to that at this time.

1006
01:25:13,185 --> 01:25:15,886
[SPEAKER_01]: They've also had to go through the MEPA review that does.

1007
01:25:16,326 --> 01:25:17,907
[SPEAKER_01]: And as part of their presentation,

1008
01:25:18,461 --> 01:25:20,382
[SPEAKER_01]: to the community development board.

1009
01:25:20,402 --> 01:25:31,965
[SPEAKER_01]: They shared with us storm level, sea level rise and storm surge maps that I am very familiar with because they were exactly the same as that we are looking at with the city.

1010
01:25:32,705 --> 01:25:43,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And so they do actually need to manage water, potential water on this site up to 2050 hundred year flood levels.

1011
01:25:43,708 --> 01:25:46,729
[SPEAKER_01]: And the cost of making their building

1012
01:25:49,011 --> 01:26:01,473
[SPEAKER_01]: resilient to that water that is going to hit this property does add to the cost of the building, which would need them to then build a larger building to be able to make it cost effective and recoup their costs.

1013
01:26:03,054 --> 01:26:09,655
[SPEAKER_01]: I am not in position to monetize that specifically, but there are a number of things that they need to do.

1014
01:26:09,775 --> 01:26:15,696
[SPEAKER_01]: And as a municipality, it is one of the things that we are looking to put into our future zoning

1015
01:26:16,153 --> 01:26:25,608
[SPEAKER_01]: because we do not want to be rescuing buildings and having to deal with buildings that have been trashed because of storm surge and sea level rise.

1016
01:26:25,628 --> 01:26:31,637
[SPEAKER_01]: I assume the board is familiar with the fact that at some point the Amelia Earhart Dam

1017
01:26:32,157 --> 01:26:34,880
[SPEAKER_01]: is going to get overtopped if we don't do something about it.

1018
01:26:35,401 --> 01:26:41,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, we have to act as if it's going to get overtopped, even though we are trying to do something about it.

1019
01:26:41,928 --> 01:26:48,014
[SPEAKER_01]: And there will be, I think the number for this location, I don't have the map in front of me, was 10 feet of

1020
01:26:49,042 --> 01:26:53,504
[SPEAKER_01]: sea level rise above ground level or of water above ground level.

1021
01:26:54,045 --> 01:26:57,126
[SPEAKER_01]: And so that's not just, oh, the building needs to be 10 feet higher.

1022
01:26:57,587 --> 01:27:08,132
[SPEAKER_01]: It means that the bottom floor of the building needs to be protected to manage being inundated with 10 feet of water, which makes it a more expensive building.

1023
01:27:08,633 --> 01:27:14,416
[SPEAKER_01]: And they may, VHB may be in a better place to speak to some of that than I actually am.

1024
01:27:15,800 --> 01:27:17,361
[SPEAKER_16]: Well, thanks for bringing that up.

1025
01:27:17,562 --> 01:27:29,170
[SPEAKER_16]: If you see the red, the red blocks on the sections, we provide additional treatments on the building that provide that water tightness above even that initial flood elevation.

1026
01:27:29,511 --> 01:27:30,952
[SPEAKER_16]: So this is still a work in progress.

1027
01:27:31,698 --> 01:27:40,204
[SPEAKER_16]: We have a lot of experience in this working on projects elsewhere in the greater Boston area that are dealing with, you know, future flood level elevation.

1028
01:27:40,244 --> 01:27:41,605
[SPEAKER_16]: So this is nothing new.

1029
01:27:41,665 --> 01:27:49,010
[SPEAKER_16]: And as this project moves forward, we have to do it for the people that are going to be in it and financing it and owning it.

1030
01:27:49,030 --> 01:27:53,033
[SPEAKER_16]: And if the town has their own regulation at some point, I think that's great.

1031
01:27:55,434 --> 01:27:55,894
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, great.

1032
01:27:55,914 --> 01:27:56,415
[SPEAKER_04]: Thanks so much.

1033
01:27:58,043 --> 01:27:59,284
[SPEAKER_04]: Mike, did you have any other questions?

1034
01:27:59,664 --> 01:28:00,744
[SPEAKER_19]: Yeah, I did.

