[SPEAKER_19]: Thanks.

1036
01:28:01,825 --> 01:28:03,786
[SPEAKER_19]: So a couple more.

1037
01:28:04,726 --> 01:28:09,848
[SPEAKER_19]: So this one, maybe Mr. Locke is the correct one to address it to.

1038
01:28:09,908 --> 01:28:10,508
[SPEAKER_19]: So we'll try.

1039
01:28:10,528 --> 01:28:18,352
[SPEAKER_19]: So the loading bays, it looks like they're interior to the building.

1040
01:28:19,072 --> 01:28:21,073
[SPEAKER_19]: That's one of the areas you're requesting a variance.

1041
01:28:21,213 --> 01:28:26,495
[SPEAKER_19]: Can you maybe just share some details as to sort of the

1042
01:28:27,660 --> 01:28:40,831
[SPEAKER_19]: the impact to the project, if we, for whatever reason, did need to enforce that, and whether you got any comments or concerns from the CDB about that particular aspect of the project.

1043
01:28:42,533 --> 01:28:43,493
[SPEAKER_17]: Yeah, absolutely.

1044
01:28:43,533 --> 01:28:44,174
[SPEAKER_17]: I'll touch on that.

1045
01:28:44,194 --> 01:28:50,019
[SPEAKER_17]: And we do have Cube 3 on the call, who is the architect behind designing the loading bays, if they want to hop in after me.

1046
01:28:50,099 --> 01:28:53,702
[SPEAKER_17]: But it's a great question, Mr. Caldera.

1047
01:28:53,722 --> 01:28:54,403
[SPEAKER_17]: Thank you so much.

1048
01:28:55,325 --> 01:29:01,069
[SPEAKER_17]: So the loading bays themselves, we do provide three onsite and the requirement is five.

1049
01:29:02,110 --> 01:29:06,233
[SPEAKER_17]: Right now they're accessed from the right-hand turn all the way back here.

1050
01:29:06,253 --> 01:29:14,259
[SPEAKER_17]: VHB did help us with providing some turning radiuses for trucks, making sure that they can fit in there and that there wouldn't be any conflicts.

1051
01:29:14,360 --> 01:29:16,381
[SPEAKER_17]: So we do have that as well.

1052
01:29:17,202 --> 01:29:22,466
[SPEAKER_17]: Really what was driving the three loading bays is the actual need for the loading bays.

1053
01:29:23,483 --> 01:29:35,933
[SPEAKER_17]: that a typical lab market, and Jacobs is also on here, who can touch on this if needed, but in the lab market, really for a building such as this, three loading bays is what is needed to support the building.

1054
01:29:36,833 --> 01:29:39,796
[SPEAKER_17]: That's what is being designed to this building.

1055
01:29:39,896 --> 01:29:50,424
[SPEAKER_17]: Five loading bays would really take up a lot of area on this site and really take away from things on the ground level that we are providing, such as these bike facilities that you see here.

1056
01:29:50,955 --> 01:29:58,137
[SPEAKER_17]: which are really part of the transportation management program, as well as some of the other ground floor space that's going to be needed to support the building.

1057
01:29:59,258 --> 01:30:03,279
[SPEAKER_17]: I'm happy to turn it over to CUBE3 if they want to talk a little bit through the design.

1058
01:30:03,699 --> 01:30:15,583
[SPEAKER_17]: But before I do that, just real quickly, we believe that three loading bays are the appropriate amount of loading bays for this site.

1059
01:30:15,883 --> 01:30:20,525
[SPEAKER_17]: If there is a need, or if there are for any reason at all, which we don't believe will happen,

1060
01:30:21,357 --> 01:30:26,180
[SPEAKER_17]: a fourth truck or a fourth vehicle coming to the site for the loading.

1061
01:30:26,961 --> 01:30:33,545
[SPEAKER_17]: The good news is we have areas that we can manage that on the site so that it is not affecting Mystic Valley Parkway.

1062
01:30:34,426 --> 01:30:37,788
[SPEAKER_17]: You know, this ring road is accessible all the way around the site.

1063
01:30:38,309 --> 01:30:44,013
[SPEAKER_17]: And most of these items that are being delivered to the site are things you could take right off the back of the truck.

1064
01:30:44,033 --> 01:30:48,536
[SPEAKER_17]: You don't really need any sort of pallets or anything like that and take right into the building.

1065
01:30:49,430 --> 01:30:57,015
[SPEAKER_17]: So I'm happy to turn it over to Cube 3 at Jacobs, if they want to elaborate a little bit more on that and give a little bit more of an expert answer.

1066
01:30:57,035 --> 01:30:58,456
[SPEAKER_17]: Thank you, Mr. Allen.

1067
01:30:58,476 --> 01:31:00,958
[SPEAKER_09]: Hi, everybody.

1068
01:31:01,398 --> 01:31:04,721
[SPEAKER_09]: Steve Presage on here, senior job captain at Cube 3 on the project.

1069
01:31:05,862 --> 01:31:09,284
[SPEAKER_09]: Not really much to elaborate on beyond the great outline Quinn just gave.

1070
01:31:09,304 --> 01:31:17,850
[SPEAKER_09]: I think all we would say is, as you can see on screen here, we're planning for three loading bays, two of which are kind of directly serving the loading dock

1071
01:31:18,245 --> 01:31:23,348
[SPEAKER_09]: central to the lab building, the third containing the trash compactor where a trash pickup would happen.

1072
01:31:23,908 --> 01:31:29,671
[SPEAKER_09]: And as Quinn mentioned, we've studied the turning radii of the trucks using a WD-40 chassis.

1073
01:31:30,612 --> 01:31:34,754
[SPEAKER_09]: So they would essentially come down the one way from page bottom here from left to right.

1074
01:31:34,914 --> 01:31:37,155
[SPEAKER_09]: They would stop and back into the loading dock.

1075
01:31:37,435 --> 01:31:42,878
[SPEAKER_09]: And we've kind of cut our curbs and given those the proper radius for trucks to be able to maneuver in and out.

1076
01:31:43,618 --> 01:31:46,540
[SPEAKER_09]: And then they would just pull straight back out to leave the site.

1077
01:31:49,772 --> 01:31:50,374
[SPEAKER_27]: Great, thank you.

1078
01:31:51,437 --> 01:31:52,219
[SPEAKER_19]: Yeah, thank you.

1079
01:31:54,180 --> 01:31:54,320
[SPEAKER_19]: Yeah.

1080
01:31:54,340 --> 01:32:05,049
[SPEAKER_19]: So just to kind of paraphrase what I'm hearing, it sounds like, um, the need for additional loading days is not really there given the use, and then it would be costly in some dimension.

1081
01:32:05,069 --> 01:32:11,274
[SPEAKER_19]: Like there'd be a, something would be something material would be given up if we had to have more loading days.

1082
01:32:11,474 --> 01:32:12,936
[SPEAKER_19]: Is that.

1083
01:32:13,376 --> 01:32:14,937
[SPEAKER_15]: Maybe go ahead, Brian.

1084
01:32:14,977 --> 01:32:15,478
[SPEAKER_15]: Yeah.

1085
01:32:15,638 --> 01:32:17,219
[SPEAKER_15]: Maybe I'll add to it is there.

1086
01:32:17,279 --> 01:32:20,402
[SPEAKER_15]: There's a couple of things fighting for the ground floor and in a lab

1087
01:32:21,152 --> 01:32:27,414
[SPEAKER_15]: Typical lab building, easily half of the ground floor would be considered back a house and it would be a chemical storage.

1088
01:32:27,454 --> 01:32:33,616
[SPEAKER_15]: It would be all of your ancillary, you know, tenant mechanical, electrical, the transformer room, everything else.

1089
01:32:34,837 --> 01:32:49,862
[SPEAKER_15]: One of the design missions that RISE had on this project was to really give a good portion of the floor to the community side facing Mystic Valley Parkway, which really limited the space that we could put the stuff essential to a lab building.

1090
01:32:50,636 --> 01:33:10,980
[SPEAKER_15]: So one of the things that we did, which you're not seeing here, but I think it's important to share, and it's also part of the resiliency, is to take some of that really sensitive infrastructure, like the electrical vault, which is a large room, and it's actually up on very valuable floor space up on the second floor, in order to make sure we preserve as much of that community site as possible.

1091
01:33:11,080 --> 01:33:19,762
[SPEAKER_15]: And the other way we did that was to, you know, get it down to what is efficient for this building to work, which was the three loading bays as shown.

1092
01:33:23,600 --> 01:33:24,300
[SPEAKER_19]: Okay, thank you.

1093
01:33:25,481 --> 01:33:30,842
[SPEAKER_19]: And then my last question, at least at this time, is definitely for Attorney Frazier.

1094
01:33:30,882 --> 01:33:39,865
[SPEAKER_19]: So I just wanted to check my understanding of kind of the argument you're making in the application.

1095
01:33:39,985 --> 01:33:48,028
[SPEAKER_19]: So it sounds like the crux of the argument as I understood it was,

1096
01:33:49,105 --> 01:34:00,290
[SPEAKER_19]: that this type of project in the current zoning, the current zoning doesn't really gel with this type of project.

1097
01:34:00,430 --> 01:34:11,114
[SPEAKER_19]: So modern lab space, especially on a lot of this size, you're gonna need much more than the two stories allowable by right.

1098
01:34:11,914 --> 01:34:12,815
[SPEAKER_19]: And so,

1099
01:34:13,735 --> 01:34:21,661
[SPEAKER_19]: So yeah, so it sounded like that was part of the argument that essentially this is the best use, but the current zoning doesn't allow it.

1100
01:34:23,642 --> 01:34:27,806
[SPEAKER_19]: And then you referenced the

1101
01:34:31,312 --> 01:34:44,806
[SPEAKER_19]: Marshallian case, which for those who aren't familiar with it, basically says that the existence of a kind of lesser use alone isn't grounds to deny a variance.

1102
01:34:44,866 --> 01:34:51,252
[SPEAKER_19]: And so as I understood that case, there was kind of a determination about

1103
01:34:52,423 --> 01:34:56,705
[SPEAKER_19]: of the other available uses, which ones were and weren't economical.

1104
01:34:56,845 --> 01:35:08,111
[SPEAKER_19]: And so I just wanted clarification from attorney Fraser about the applicant stance on these other uses and why.

1105
01:35:08,671 --> 01:35:15,175
[SPEAKER_19]: So clearly this is highest and best use, but why other alternatives aren't appropriate for this lot.

1106
01:35:16,545 --> 01:35:27,174
[SPEAKER_21]: Thank you, Mr. Caldera and the question that you asked actually goes to the heart of the Mount Rushmore case, which is that just because there are other ways that the lot could be used.

1107
01:35:28,035 --> 01:35:29,917
[SPEAKER_21]: It doesn't mean that there's not a hardship.

1108
01:35:30,797 --> 01:35:40,726
[SPEAKER_21]: So, so sure they know in that case there with the plaintiff argued that the lot could be used for this or the lot could be used for that, so that there's no need to grant the

1109
01:35:43,132 --> 01:35:51,981
[SPEAKER_21]: The court said in that case was that was exactly the proposition that you're stating is that even though the lot could could be used for for other purposes.

1110
01:35:53,222 --> 01:35:57,367
[SPEAKER_21]: It can still be considered a hardship if you can't be brought to its highest and best use.

1111
01:35:59,826 --> 01:36:13,644
[SPEAKER_19]: Okay, yeah, I my understanding is similar and disclaimer I'm not a lawyer so I'll kind of take your interpretation over mine but I did get the impression that it went a step further and so it wasn't just that the.

1112
01:36:14,612 --> 01:36:16,533
[SPEAKER_19]: plaintiff's argument was kind of struck down.

1113
01:36:16,573 --> 01:36:22,775
[SPEAKER_19]: It was that the other uses that they proposed were in fact not economical.

1114
01:36:22,995 --> 01:36:26,496
[SPEAKER_19]: So I just was seeking clarification.

1115
01:36:26,516 --> 01:36:33,979
[SPEAKER_19]: Is it your stance that other uses are not just not highest and best, but are also not economical for this lot?

1116
01:36:35,691 --> 01:36:51,643
[SPEAKER_21]: Not economical for the lot, and also because of the character of the lot, which is another criteria that was used in that Marsharp-Braxley case, as far as topography and other characteristics of the lot also created a hardship in that case, as they do here.

1117
01:36:51,663 --> 01:36:53,705
[SPEAKER_21]: So that would be our second argument as well.

1118
01:36:54,846 --> 01:36:55,526
[SPEAKER_19]: OK, thank you.

1119
01:36:55,766 --> 01:36:57,367
[SPEAKER_19]: I've asked all my questions at this time.

1120
01:36:58,768 --> 01:36:59,689
[SPEAKER_27]: Thanks, Mike.

1121
01:36:59,709 --> 01:37:00,270
[SPEAKER_27]: Jim, are you back?

1122
01:37:01,651 --> 01:37:03,392
[SPEAKER_14]: I don't have any at this time.

1123
01:37:06,060 --> 01:37:06,820
[SPEAKER_14]: I have no questions.

1124
01:37:08,041 --> 01:37:08,301
[SPEAKER_27]: Okay.

1125
01:37:09,962 --> 01:37:16,926
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, so I, um, I usually have a lot of questions, particular, particularly for larger projects like this.

1126
01:37:17,727 --> 01:37:23,690
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, but I guess just to start, I want to say that I really appreciate that the application that we've gotten is quite comprehensive.

1127
01:37:24,431 --> 01:37:27,933
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, and in reading through that, a lot of my questions, um, were answered.

1128
01:37:28,793 --> 01:37:29,674
[SPEAKER_04]: I even think, um,

1129
01:37:30,205 --> 01:37:49,073
[SPEAKER_04]: When I first read through some of the answers, I was a little concerned because I wasn't seeing reference to the conditions that are required for us to even go through the structural and the statutory analysis for variants, but that was addressed in Attorney Frazier's letter that we received as well.

1130
01:37:49,473 --> 01:37:58,357
[SPEAKER_04]: So I don't really have any questions that are still open, but I just wanted to sort of check in with everybody who may be watching.

1131
01:37:58,936 --> 01:38:09,383
[SPEAKER_04]: We talk about this all the time, but as a refresher, going through for a variance, we need to make sure that as the board, we're looking at some statutory criteria.

1132
01:38:09,403 --> 01:38:13,826
[SPEAKER_04]: There need to be circumstances relating to soil condition, shape and topography.

1133
01:38:14,647 --> 01:38:21,051
[SPEAKER_04]: We've heard the applicant speak regarding the narrowness and width of the site, topography and slope.

1134
01:38:21,091 --> 01:38:23,853
[SPEAKER_04]: We've also heard about wetlands and storm surges.

1135
01:38:24,955 --> 01:38:46,325
[SPEAKER_04]: Those circumstances need to be particular to this parcel, unique to the parcel, where the literal enforcement would create a hardship, which we've heard the applicant speak to some of the issues regarding the way that modern labs are built, the shape of them, the modularity of them.

1136
01:38:49,006 --> 01:38:50,247
[SPEAKER_04]: the restrictions that we have.

1137
01:38:50,647 --> 01:39:10,107
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm particularly compelled when I look at this project that this is, you know, Marcelline gets to our hardship issue, right, not to our condition issue, but our hardship issue about the highest and best use, but particularly that this is an allowed use, but the modern use of it would not really be otherwise economical or feasible.

1138
01:39:10,590 --> 01:39:26,734
[SPEAKER_04]: Unless as the applicant pointed out in their application, you had a lot, I forget what the number was something like eight or nine times the size of this one, which really isn't feasible to be asking any applicants to come in and it would almost prohibit the use of this, or rather the construction of this use at all.

1139
01:39:29,740 --> 01:39:37,906
[SPEAKER_04]: I always forget the exact language in the last one, but that the relief requested can be granted without substantial detriment or without nullifying the bond.

1140
01:39:37,926 --> 01:39:42,229
[SPEAKER_04]: So for anyone listening, those are the four criteria that we need to take a look at.

1141
01:39:43,931 --> 01:39:47,873
[SPEAKER_04]: And in this, there are separate criteria for site planning we'll get into in a moment.

1142
01:39:47,913 --> 01:39:54,678
[SPEAKER_04]: But from what I'm seeing, I'm quite pleased both with what's been provided, the thoroughness of the application.

1143
01:39:54,819 --> 01:39:59,302
[SPEAKER_04]: And also I particularly wanna call out the community engagement that we've seen on this.

1144
01:40:00,443 --> 01:40:01,463
[SPEAKER_04]: Mr. Schrader was right.

1145
01:40:02,604 --> 01:40:11,006
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't often see projects that come in this lotted by City Hall, by the public, neighborhood meetings where there's almost no opposition.

1146
01:40:13,247 --> 01:40:22,030
[SPEAKER_04]: This project comes to us and I can tell that the planning and development of it has been very thoughtful and particularly just appreciate that engagement with the community.

1147
01:40:22,250 --> 01:40:25,731
[SPEAKER_04]: So those are my thoughts where we are on the hardship portion.

1148
01:40:27,015 --> 01:40:32,216
[SPEAKER_04]: I think it's probably appropriate now if the applicant wants to run through their presentation on site plan review.

1149
01:40:34,897 --> 01:40:35,917
[SPEAKER_17]: Thank you, Madam Chairwoman.

1150
01:40:35,977 --> 01:40:36,457
[SPEAKER_17]: Absolutely.

1151
01:40:36,977 --> 01:40:39,978
[SPEAKER_17]: We're going to go ahead and get started with the special permit criteria.

1152
01:40:40,058 --> 01:40:44,899
[SPEAKER_17]: So there are seven special permit criteria that we've been focusing on.

1153
01:40:45,739 --> 01:40:48,800
[SPEAKER_17]: And I'll have the teams run through each one of them individually.

1154
01:40:49,400 --> 01:40:53,541
[SPEAKER_17]: We're going to go ahead and start with our transportation team.

1155
01:40:53,581 --> 01:40:55,461
[SPEAKER_17]: Ryan White should be on the call from DHB.

1156
01:40:58,537 --> 01:41:10,947
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, and you know what, actually, before you do that, just as an explanation to everyone listening again, so site plan, this project, they need to get a couple of variances, which is what I was just explaining that criteria.

1157
01:41:11,407 --> 01:41:14,089
[SPEAKER_04]: Site plan review has a whole other set of criteria.

1158
01:41:15,530 --> 01:41:24,497
[SPEAKER_04]: And the board has the power to make that finding provided that the seven criteria

1159
01:41:27,689 --> 01:41:35,453
[SPEAKER_04]: we can approve the site plan unless we find that some of the seven criteria are in play.

1160
01:41:35,833 --> 01:41:37,934
[SPEAKER_04]: So it sounds like they're about to go through those seven.

1161
01:41:38,414 --> 01:41:39,354
[SPEAKER_04]: Pardon the interruption, go ahead.

1162
01:41:40,415 --> 01:41:41,556
[SPEAKER_17]: Thank you, Madam Chairwoman.

1163
01:41:42,356 --> 01:41:48,319
[SPEAKER_17]: As said, I think Ryan White is on the call to start us off with some of the transportation items that you see here.

1164
01:41:49,370 --> 01:41:49,870
[SPEAKER_22]: Sounds good.

1165
01:41:50,491 --> 01:41:51,131
[SPEAKER_22]: Thanks, Quinn.

1166
01:41:51,352 --> 01:41:52,793
[SPEAKER_22]: And hello, members of the board.

1167
01:41:53,313 --> 01:41:54,094
[SPEAKER_22]: My name is Ryan White.

1168
01:41:54,134 --> 01:41:58,357
[SPEAKER_22]: I'm a project manager and senior transportation engineer with VHB.

1169
01:41:58,937 --> 01:42:03,161
[SPEAKER_22]: And I led the transportation and traffic elements of this project.

1170
01:42:03,881 --> 01:42:09,305
[SPEAKER_22]: I personally am going to talk to two of the elements really regarding site access to this parcel.

1171
01:42:10,206 --> 01:42:12,448
[SPEAKER_22]: So first, let's talk about criteria one.

1172
01:42:13,077 --> 01:42:21,002
[SPEAKER_22]: and the proposed improvements to the site that will really improve safety and access from the existing conditions that are out there today.

1173
01:42:21,823 --> 01:42:30,469
[SPEAKER_22]: This includes eliminating one site curb cut while the other two are both narrowed and both become one way rather than full access.

1174
01:42:31,089 --> 01:42:40,576
[SPEAKER_22]: A site distance study was conducted to ensure that there's ample visibility between both vehicles and pedestrians, which was included in the project's transportation impact study

1175
01:42:41,162 --> 01:42:43,524
[SPEAKER_22]: which has been reviewed by the city's traffic engineer.

1176
01:42:45,145 --> 01:42:52,029
[SPEAKER_22]: Parking has been moved from the existing surface lot that's there to the underground parking garage.

1177
01:42:52,790 --> 01:43:00,175
[SPEAKER_22]: Loading, as we mentioned, will incur internal to the building and will be accessed directly from that one-way loop road.

1178
01:43:01,255 --> 01:43:07,740
[SPEAKER_22]: Additionally, as mitigation, the project has committed to working with MassDOT to study

1179
01:43:08,310 --> 01:43:21,218
[SPEAKER_22]: And if feasible, design and construct a mid-block crossing here and initial concepts on the right to better connect this parcel with the park and hopefully with Wellington Station in the future with other projects planned by the city.

1180
01:43:21,998 --> 01:43:31,224
[SPEAKER_22]: DOT was generally receptive to this improvement during the initial site permitting meetings, but we'll have to do more in-depth studies.

1181
01:43:31,840 --> 01:43:36,881
[SPEAKER_22]: to validate this concept is obviously Mystic Valley Parkways under DOT jurisdiction.

1182
01:43:37,742 --> 01:43:53,306
[SPEAKER_22]: To improve bike conditions and access to this building and site as well, the proponents working with the city to sponsor Blue Bike Station on or near the site, and we'll again work with DOT about the possibility of a bike facility along Mystic Valley Parkway.

1183
01:43:54,866 --> 01:43:56,667
[SPEAKER_22]: Quinn, could you go on to the next slide?

1184
01:43:57,290 --> 01:44:06,780
[SPEAKER_22]: Just in an interest of keeping presenters consistent, I'm going to fast forward and talk about criteria six while I have the screen right now.

1185
01:44:07,641 --> 01:44:12,006
[SPEAKER_22]: And really what I want to focus on as part of this discussion is the one-way loop road itself.

1186
01:44:13,187 --> 01:44:27,958
[SPEAKER_22]: First of all, by making it one-way loop road, we will decrease the number of vehicle conflicts with the sidewalk, as well as simplify driver expectations along Mystic Valley Parkway, hopefully creating a much safer condition than what's out there right now.

1187
01:44:29,239 --> 01:44:38,005
[SPEAKER_22]: The onsite drive aisle will provide vehicular access for all portions of the site, the drop-off area, the loading docks, the parking garage,

1188
01:44:38,445 --> 01:44:53,289
[SPEAKER_22]: But additionally, and probably most importantly, the loop road has been designed for emergency and fire access, and has been designed, as previously mentioned, to allow a Medford city fire truck to fully access all sides of the building.

1189
01:44:54,269 --> 01:45:01,271
[SPEAKER_22]: And with that, let me hand it over to my colleague, Mark, who will talk more about some of the other criteria related to the onsite infrastructure.

1190
01:45:03,172 --> 01:45:06,593
[SPEAKER_16]: All right, I'm gonna rewind to criteria two,

1191
01:45:07,725 --> 01:45:16,905
[SPEAKER_16]: which talks about the configuration of the project and its paved areas, whether it has a detrimental effect on adjacent properties or the infrastructure.

1192
01:45:18,172 --> 01:45:34,082
[SPEAKER_16]: We talked a little bit about the drainage system previously, that we're taking a site that has no drainage controls and providing kind of all the latest and greatest drainage controls, including the last piece of our drainage system is a filter.

1193
01:45:34,163 --> 01:45:40,266
[SPEAKER_16]: It's a filter with replaceable cartridges that kind of polishes the stormwater before it goes out to the wetland.

1194
01:45:41,247 --> 01:45:45,630
[SPEAKER_16]: We feel that the drainage system will not only control the peaks off the site, you know,

1195
01:45:46,373 --> 01:45:58,386
[SPEAKER_16]: bring it back to something a little more reasonable, but it will also greatly increase the water quality going into the wetland, because this wetland that's behind the site is, you know, an important piece of the local ecosystem.

1196
01:45:58,907 --> 01:45:59,928
[SPEAKER_16]: And we recognize that.

1197
01:46:01,069 --> 01:46:06,415
[SPEAKER_16]: The site does not have wetland resource areas on it, just adjacent on the other properties.

1198
01:46:07,172 --> 01:46:15,560
[SPEAKER_16]: And we've designed around that and we will be going to the Conservation Commission to have a discussion about our proximity to that.

1199
01:46:15,660 --> 01:46:19,543
[SPEAKER_16]: And I'm sure they'll have some things that they want to make sure that we're doing.

1200
01:46:19,583 --> 01:46:21,966
[SPEAKER_16]: And I think we're in a good place on that.

1201
01:46:23,487 --> 01:46:29,953
[SPEAKER_16]: We have water services, two water services available out front in Mystic Valley Parkway.

1202
01:46:30,909 --> 01:46:38,436
[SPEAKER_16]: and we have a reasonably large collector sewer available in Mystic Valley Parkway as well.

1203
01:46:38,496 --> 01:46:44,160
[SPEAKER_16]: So access to those utilities is easily achieved without impacts to our neighbors.

1204
01:46:45,722 --> 01:46:49,245
[SPEAKER_16]: And the other thing about the drainage on the site is it is all self-contained.

1205
01:46:49,285 --> 01:46:53,388
[SPEAKER_16]: We don't go through someone else's site to access that low area.

1206
01:46:53,909 --> 01:46:56,471
[SPEAKER_16]: So we're not going through an existing drainage pipe and

1207
01:46:57,131 --> 01:47:00,794
[SPEAKER_16]: changing that parameter as part of this project.

1208
01:47:02,255 --> 01:47:07,378
[SPEAKER_16]: So well served by utilities because we have pretty much everything we need in Mystic Valley Parkway or on the site.

1209
01:47:08,920 --> 01:47:16,085
[SPEAKER_16]: Criteria four talks a little bit more about the relationship to natural resources.

1210
01:47:16,165 --> 01:47:25,992
[SPEAKER_16]: And maybe this is a little bit of repeating here, sorry, because not only does the drainage not have impacts on our neighbors, it also doesn't have impacts on the existing

1211
01:47:27,157 --> 01:47:32,502
[SPEAKER_16]: resources, which in this case are the wetland areas, which I kind of touched on already.

1212
01:47:33,403 --> 01:47:48,877
[SPEAKER_16]: So where our site is pretty much paved right now, there's only a slight change, if not exactly the same impervious area in the proposed condition as the existing, so we're not really impacting

1213
01:47:50,653 --> 01:47:59,597
[SPEAKER_16]: on site natural resources, if you want to call you know sort of the existing landscape that's out there actually will have great improvement on the landscape and we're done.

1214
01:47:59,617 --> 01:48:09,941
[SPEAKER_16]: And I think that covers criteria for next is criteria three which will be handled by landscape architect Mike.

1215
01:48:11,262 --> 01:48:11,662
[SPEAKER_18]: Thanks mark.

1216
01:48:12,130 --> 01:48:13,631
[SPEAKER_18]: Hi, my name is Mike Rettmeyer.

1217
01:48:13,671 --> 01:48:17,154
[SPEAKER_18]: I'm a landscape architect at Michael D'Angelo Landscape Architecture.

1218
01:48:17,915 --> 01:48:20,457
[SPEAKER_18]: I'm going to hit on criteria number three and five.

1219
01:48:21,778 --> 01:48:25,921
[SPEAKER_18]: Criteria three is in regard to landscaping and protection of the neighbors.

1220
01:48:27,382 --> 01:48:41,393
[SPEAKER_18]: There's been some general discussion about site design and what's being proposed for the landscape, but starting at Mystic Valley Parkway, our goal is to preserve and enhance the public realm and street furniture along the parkway.

1221
01:48:41,837 --> 01:48:47,602
[SPEAKER_18]: So we're keeping the existing street trees as well as adding a new street tree to continue that rhythm.

1222
01:48:48,083 --> 01:48:52,307
[SPEAKER_18]: The lawn shoulder is being renovated as well as a new concrete sidewalk.

1223
01:48:53,891 --> 01:49:03,621
[SPEAKER_18]: with the new public entry plaza that leads to the main entry, the community space on the ground floor, as well as the cafe space.

1224
01:49:05,623 --> 01:49:08,686
[SPEAKER_18]: The focus was for the community and for it to be open to the public.

1225
01:49:09,486 --> 01:49:16,193
[SPEAKER_18]: There's a generous landscape buffer with shade trees, perennial shrubs, as well as rain

1226
01:49:16,753 --> 01:49:18,555
[SPEAKER_18]: rain garden for stormwater management.

1227
01:49:19,015 --> 01:49:34,330
[SPEAKER_18]: The plaza is designed with flexibility in mind for, you know, with different seating options, whether it's for the cafe or people spilling out from the community space or within the lab building itself.

1228
01:49:35,371 --> 01:49:40,757
[SPEAKER_18]: But with all those seating, the seating being flexible, it also allows for events to be held in this public plaza as well.

1229
01:49:44,184 --> 01:49:51,947
[SPEAKER_18]: The planting design in general does consist of native and adapted plant materials to help increase biodiversity, provide shade.

1230
01:49:53,635 --> 01:49:55,116
[SPEAKER_18]: and seasonal interest throughout the year.

1231
01:49:56,197 --> 01:50:02,081
[SPEAKER_18]: Along the perimeter of the site is a retaining wall that's been mentioned earlier in the presentation.

1232
01:50:03,322 --> 01:50:07,866
[SPEAKER_18]: As that wall gets higher towards the back of the site, a guardrail is required.

1233
01:50:09,167 --> 01:50:19,975
[SPEAKER_18]: And with keeping in mind the views from neighboring properties, we'll be designing that wall and the guardrail in a decorative way to make sure any views

1234
01:50:21,185 --> 01:50:28,551
[SPEAKER_18]: looking onto our property are positive to represent the rest of the project and the architecture.

1235
01:50:29,372 --> 01:50:36,537
[SPEAKER_18]: At the north part of the site, we've maximized the amount of planting in that area.

1236
01:50:36,577 --> 01:50:44,423
[SPEAKER_18]: This is all along the wetland buffer as well, just to add back as much green space and habitat creation as we can.

1237
01:50:45,064 --> 01:50:49,447
[SPEAKER_18]: And again, in terms of circulation, there is along the loop road and

1238
01:50:51,372 --> 01:51:10,898
[SPEAKER_18]: the side property lines, we are providing decorative paving along the outer curb line for pedestrian use, and as well as to emphasize the decorative paving at the main entries coming off of Mystic Valley Parkway.

1239
01:51:10,938 --> 01:51:11,818
[SPEAKER_18]: Next slide, Quinn.

1240
01:51:13,376 --> 01:51:17,559
[SPEAKER_18]: is criteria number five, which is in regard to the exterior lighting.

1241
01:51:17,579 --> 01:51:25,646
[SPEAKER_18]: All of our site lighting, and it's all downward-directed light to limit light pollution.

1242
01:51:26,847 --> 01:51:32,952
[SPEAKER_18]: We have run photometrics for this project, and there's zero light spill over the property lines.

1243
01:51:36,372 --> 01:51:38,715
[SPEAKER_18]: There's three different types of fixtures that we've included.

1244
01:51:39,476 --> 01:51:52,893
[SPEAKER_18]: The fixtures that are directly mounted, they're building mounted fixtures around the perimeter of the building to safely illuminate the loop drive as well as the pedestrian sidewalks on the building.

1245
01:51:53,434 --> 01:51:57,576
[SPEAKER_18]: The entered plaza includes bollard lights and then decorative light poles.

1246
01:51:59,057 --> 01:52:03,979
[SPEAKER_18]: Again, just to create safe light levels for pedestrians visiting the site.

1247
01:52:04,420 --> 01:52:09,142
[SPEAKER_18]: And I do want to note that the existing parkway streetlights are to remain.

1248
01:52:09,923 --> 01:52:12,904
[SPEAKER_18]: So that'll function as currently designed along the parkway.

1249
01:52:14,205 --> 01:52:16,426
[SPEAKER_18]: And with that, I'll turn it back to Quinn.

1250
01:52:20,463 --> 01:52:20,963
[SPEAKER_17]: Thank you, Mike.

1251
01:52:21,043 --> 01:52:26,705
[SPEAKER_17]: And for the last criteria, criteria number seven, really focusing on waste management on the site.

1252
01:52:27,105 --> 01:52:37,109
[SPEAKER_17]: We did want to present both construction waste management plan that will be created in parallel with some of these soil management plans.

1253
01:52:37,489 --> 01:52:45,292
