[SPEAKER_05]: It's just to open the hearing.

1780
02:30:57,274 --> 02:30:59,055
[SPEAKER_05]: So basically we get it, we have to have it.

1781
02:30:59,075 --> 02:31:01,017
[SPEAKER_05]: And so, and with the issue with, uh,

1782
02:31:02,317 --> 02:31:12,021
[SPEAKER_05]: like legal notices and mailings and things like that, it pretty much pushes us to, you know, the timing wise, it's very, very tight window as to when we can actually have the hearing.

1783
02:31:12,041 --> 02:31:22,685
[SPEAKER_05]: So, I mean, you know, I was sort of hoping, hopefully we could maybe talk to them and have them actually submit it on a day that would actually allow us to have it on our regularly scheduled hearing, but that may not be possible.

1784
02:31:23,465 --> 02:31:25,186
[SPEAKER_04]: That would be amazing if it happened though.

1785
02:31:25,446 --> 02:31:26,547
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

1786
02:31:28,159 --> 02:31:30,440
[SPEAKER_05]: And we're also completely unrelated.

1787
02:31:30,500 --> 02:31:30,881
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, sorry.

1788
02:31:31,421 --> 02:31:32,161
[SPEAKER_10]: Sorry, Dennis.

1789
02:31:32,302 --> 02:31:33,182
[SPEAKER_05]: Apologize.

1790
02:31:33,883 --> 02:31:36,484
[SPEAKER_05]: No, this is genuinely just for my own sanity.

1791
02:31:37,124 --> 02:31:40,006
[SPEAKER_05]: Anyone else living near Redford Square hear fireworks for the last half hour?

1792
02:31:42,568 --> 02:31:43,088
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay, good.

1793
02:31:43,188 --> 02:31:45,389
[SPEAKER_05]: All right.

1794
02:31:45,450 --> 02:31:47,151
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, it's been going on for a while.

1795
02:31:47,351 --> 02:31:50,012
[SPEAKER_05]: And so I'm just making sure that there wasn't something else happening.

1796
02:31:50,032 --> 02:31:53,414
[SPEAKER_10]: So that is I'm in Cambridge and I'm hearing them.

1797
02:31:53,875 --> 02:31:55,035
[SPEAKER_10]: I think it must be okay.

1798
02:31:55,055 --> 02:31:55,916
[SPEAKER_10]: Maybe it's encore.

1799
02:31:57,173 --> 02:31:58,274
[SPEAKER_07]: Oh, okay.

1800
02:31:59,816 --> 02:32:01,077
[SPEAKER_10]: Okay, good.

1801
02:32:01,137 --> 02:32:18,355
[SPEAKER_10]: Back to the 40Bs, we are planning to invite the state's kind of 40B zoning consultant to do a quick education for the board on

1802
02:32:19,422 --> 02:32:22,145
[SPEAKER_10]: the 40 B process and your role.

1803
02:32:22,585 --> 02:32:27,090
[SPEAKER_10]: Um, so I think that'll happen at this same time as the hearing.

1804
02:32:27,110 --> 02:32:31,995
[SPEAKER_10]: Um, but Alicia, I know you've had more with this, right?

1805
02:32:32,115 --> 02:32:36,619
[SPEAKER_01]: So last time we did this two and a half years ago and there were different members of the board.

1806
02:32:36,759 --> 02:32:39,282
[SPEAKER_01]: So it seemed to us, we really ought to do another training.

1807
02:32:39,700 --> 02:32:44,124
[SPEAKER_01]: because the 40B process is different from regular zoning board stuff.

1808
02:32:44,804 --> 02:32:47,186
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why they do separate training for it.

1809
02:32:48,047 --> 02:32:52,470
[SPEAKER_01]: So we have a consultant that's paid for by the state to guide us with it.

1810
02:32:52,871 --> 02:32:56,253
[SPEAKER_01]: We also have legal counsel from KP Law.

1811
02:32:56,714 --> 02:33:07,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So the lawyer who's been the lawyer all along on this has a ton of experience with 40Bs, not just fighting against them, but working with them and helping them work through the system.

1812
02:33:07,516 --> 02:33:11,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And she and the consultant have actually done this together many times.

1813
02:33:12,743 --> 02:33:16,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So they're basically saying they think they should do another training.

1814
02:33:17,549 --> 02:33:23,297
[SPEAKER_01]: What we did last time was that it was a one hour training and then we had the meeting.

1815
02:33:23,780 --> 02:33:32,150
[SPEAKER_01]: And in that case that ZBA never heard the case they immediately voted to put to invoke glam.

1816
02:33:32,790 --> 02:33:38,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously the whole point of the remand is that we are not invoking glam we are actually hearing the case this time.

1817
02:33:39,990 --> 02:33:47,955
[SPEAKER_01]: So I actually don't necessarily recommend doing it on a regular board night because you don't want to have other hearings.

1818
02:33:48,215 --> 02:33:58,322
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to have other cases that night because it would make sense unless you want to do the training completely separate on a completely separate night and then have the hearing.

1819
02:33:58,782 --> 02:34:02,285
[SPEAKER_01]: But you basically they're going to want to present a large project.

1820
02:34:03,105 --> 02:34:06,888
[SPEAKER_01]: What is extremely typical and what the consultants will tell you is that

1821
02:34:07,988 --> 02:34:18,091
[SPEAKER_01]: we would then hire peer reviewers to peer review traffic, all the various things, rather than just using our own in-house people.

1822
02:34:18,792 --> 02:34:23,633
[SPEAKER_01]: The applicant has to pay for those peer reviewers, so it's no additional cost to the city.

1823
02:34:24,494 --> 02:34:30,015
[SPEAKER_01]: And there are a number of peer reviewers who do this frequently for 40B projects specifically.

1824
02:34:30,435 --> 02:34:34,917
[SPEAKER_01]: And so the consultants already have, like our consultants have recommendations

1825
02:34:35,548 --> 02:34:38,015
[SPEAKER_01]: on what firms to use for those peer reviews.

1826
02:34:38,556 --> 02:34:42,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So the typical process would be that the first night you'll hear

1827
02:34:42,820 --> 02:34:44,161
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll present their project.

1828
02:34:44,461 --> 02:34:47,062
[SPEAKER_01]: It'll actually be the first time they present it to the public.

1829
02:34:47,562 --> 02:34:55,627
[SPEAKER_01]: You may want to ask some clarifying questions or just generally understand it, but you won't be asked to make any decisions immediately.

1830
02:34:56,067 --> 02:35:02,810
[SPEAKER_01]: What we would, what would be recommended to you is that you'll say, and now we would like our peer review consultants to look at it.

1831
02:35:03,471 --> 02:35:08,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And I've been told that usually you would give the peer reviewers two months to do that.

1832
02:35:08,490 --> 02:35:16,812
[SPEAKER_01]: And so you would then also schedule for a date certain that's about two months later, at which point you'll have back your peer reviews.

1833
02:35:17,312 --> 02:35:20,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when you'll start to actually think very critically about the project.

1834
02:35:21,413 --> 02:35:25,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that helpful in setting a timeline and expectations?

1835
02:35:26,975 --> 02:35:29,795
[SPEAKER_26]: Yeah, very much so.

1836
02:35:30,435 --> 02:35:36,717
[SPEAKER_04]: So I guess we'll just have to see when it comes in and then maybe everyone can just sort of think about in terms of

1837
02:35:37,154 --> 02:35:41,216
[SPEAKER_04]: how we want to structure it, training, regular meeting, separate meeting, together, different.

1838
02:35:43,178 --> 02:35:47,380
[SPEAKER_10]: Yeah, why don't we send out an email once we have a better idea of the date.

1839
02:35:47,660 --> 02:35:51,083
[SPEAKER_10]: And if anyone has any opinions, please let us know.

1840
02:35:51,103 --> 02:35:56,106
[SPEAKER_10]: We can work this however would be most helpful for you all.

1841
02:35:56,286 --> 02:36:03,150
[SPEAKER_10]: But once we have a better idea of the timeline, then we can start to build in what happens when.

1842
02:36:04,633 --> 02:36:18,982
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, and possibly if we're just doing the training for the board, maybe we do the training with Robin and on the 40B at the same time.

1843
02:36:19,742 --> 02:36:20,042
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know.

1844
02:36:20,242 --> 02:36:20,703
[SPEAKER_04]: That might be.

1845
02:36:23,724 --> 02:36:26,286
[SPEAKER_25]: Well, wouldn't we want to get the training with Robin done before the next meeting?

1846
02:36:27,940 --> 02:36:31,321
[SPEAKER_25]: It doesn't sound like he's going to be in for the July meeting.

1847
02:36:31,942 --> 02:36:37,444
[SPEAKER_04]: Just thinking in terms of I know summer scheduling can be tough for people, but I don't have a particular preference.

1848
02:36:38,884 --> 02:36:43,246
[SPEAKER_19]: I think we should tie out over email summer as called out summer scheduling is tough.

1849
02:36:43,506 --> 02:36:46,487
[SPEAKER_19]: Yeah, you know, I'd be happy to actually have.

1850
02:36:47,434 --> 02:36:50,675
[SPEAKER_19]: each of them be distinct things, as long as we planned enough in advance.

1851
02:36:50,735 --> 02:36:53,776
[SPEAKER_19]: But if we want to consolidate, that's fine, too.

1852
02:36:54,696 --> 02:36:55,316
[SPEAKER_19]: I agree.

1853
02:36:55,416 --> 02:37:06,219
[SPEAKER_19]: Ideally, if we could find a time before our next meeting to help our newer board members, those of us who haven't experienced it before, I think that would be great.

1854
02:37:06,239 --> 02:37:08,580
[SPEAKER_19]: Yeah, I tend to share

1855
02:37:10,280 --> 02:37:34,008
[SPEAKER_19]: Alicia's sentiments that it probably makes sense for that first 40B hearing to be its own standalone just so we don't have what happened today where it's like there's some smaller projects that we have to take out of order and then it's going to eat up you know however many minutes or hours and then you go into it right so if it's a special meeting it's standalone

1856
02:37:35,767 --> 02:37:40,029
[SPEAKER_19]: It's a little odd to me that would consolidate the training with that, but I don't have a strong preference.

1857
02:37:40,069 --> 02:37:44,651
[SPEAKER_19]: So I think we can just maybe discuss over email, see what dates work and figure it out.

1858
02:37:45,312 --> 02:37:47,092
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, let's do email.

1859
02:37:47,693 --> 02:37:49,954
[SPEAKER_04]: We'll just try and figure out what's easiest for people to schedule.

1860
02:37:51,335 --> 02:38:03,700
[SPEAKER_10]: And just an update on one of the other 4BBs, the Davis Company's proposal for Felsway, they will be holding a neighborhood meeting on July 11th.

1861
02:38:05,468 --> 02:38:12,811
[SPEAKER_10]: We've asked them to just hold a meeting, walk the community through their project.

1862
02:38:13,871 --> 02:38:21,394
[SPEAKER_10]: We have not been talking to them other than to request that they do this, and that's on July 11th.

1863
02:38:21,434 --> 02:38:22,974
[SPEAKER_10]: So you're certainly welcome to attend.

1864
02:38:23,375 --> 02:38:31,177
[SPEAKER_10]: We're robocalling the entire community about this and firing abutters, 400-foot abutters as well.

1865
02:38:31,217 --> 02:38:32,258
[SPEAKER_10]: So we're really trying to push

1866
02:38:33,809 --> 02:38:35,430
[SPEAKER_10]: the word out that this is happening.

1867
02:38:36,270 --> 02:38:36,750
[SPEAKER_26]: Okay, great.

1868
02:38:38,750 --> 02:38:39,951
[SPEAKER_26]: Sounds good.

1869
02:38:41,251 --> 02:38:43,492
[SPEAKER_26]: Okay, do we have anything else to go over?

1870
02:38:44,012 --> 02:38:45,252
[SPEAKER_26]: Or should we vote to adjourn?

1871
02:38:45,272 --> 02:38:50,034
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think we actually- Can we confirm our next meeting?

1872
02:38:50,054 --> 02:38:51,514
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, good call.

1873
02:38:53,475 --> 02:38:57,075
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's on the 28th, I'm out.

1874
02:38:59,376 --> 02:39:00,036
[SPEAKER_26]: Good news.

1875
02:39:00,797 --> 02:39:02,437
[SPEAKER_26]: We have associate members now.

1876
02:39:06,040 --> 02:39:08,181
[SPEAKER_04]: Or we will have people sworn in by that time.

1877
02:39:10,642 --> 02:39:11,923
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, 28th is fine with me.

1878
02:39:15,084 --> 02:39:17,965
[SPEAKER_19]: I'm pretty sure it's fine with me.

1879
02:39:22,227 --> 02:39:24,628
[SPEAKER_10]: Jamie and Talia, not every meeting goes to 1030.

1880
02:39:24,888 --> 02:39:27,549
[SPEAKER_10]: They go later.

1881
02:39:27,729 --> 02:39:28,329
[SPEAKER_07]: No, just kidding.

1882
02:39:28,349 --> 02:39:29,030
[SPEAKER_10]: They go later.

1883
02:39:29,050 --> 02:39:29,850
[SPEAKER_10]: 1230, grab some coffee.

1884
02:39:32,930 --> 02:39:39,455
[SPEAKER_04]: When I first got on the board, I feel like we were going into like after 10 every time and I was like, what have I done?

1885
02:39:39,475 --> 02:39:41,877
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I don't know about that.

1886
02:39:41,937 --> 02:39:43,678
[SPEAKER_04]: I feel like I had the same experience.

1887
02:39:45,940 --> 02:39:51,104
[SPEAKER_01]: I will say that this board is scheduled meetings and meets very specifically.

1888
02:39:51,773 --> 02:39:58,756
[SPEAKER_01]: but for some boards like the CD board was having trouble with it was just too late and they couldn't make smart decisions.

1889
02:39:59,316 --> 02:40:07,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So we asked them if as members, they preferred more meetings that were shorter or to keep to once a month and longer.

1890
02:40:08,140 --> 02:40:15,943
[SPEAKER_01]: And they decided that they would prefer to have two meetings in a month if it meant that each meeting was a shorter meeting.

1891
02:40:16,483 --> 02:40:18,785
[SPEAKER_01]: and they weren't going really late at night.

1892
02:40:19,285 --> 02:40:22,468
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'm not necessarily suggesting like that at this point.

1893
02:40:22,648 --> 02:40:27,952
[SPEAKER_01]: It may frankly become necessary with 40 Bs if those meetings tend to be long.

1894
02:40:29,053 --> 02:40:37,399
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is at the discretion of the board if you would prefer to meet more frequently in order to not have them go so late.

1895
02:40:40,602 --> 02:40:42,463
[SPEAKER_04]: Tell me anything about...

1896
02:40:44,152 --> 02:40:48,054
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, if we come to that point of necessity, I'm obviously I'll do what's best for the board.

1897
02:40:48,794 --> 02:40:51,315
[SPEAKER_04]: Thursdays are kind of tough.

1898
02:40:51,955 --> 02:40:56,877
[SPEAKER_04]: It seems like we got the last day available for these meetings of all after everyone else got the pick.

1899
02:40:57,837 --> 02:41:02,559
[SPEAKER_04]: So having two Thursdays a month, I think would be a bit tough for me, but we'll do what we need to.

1900
02:41:04,230 --> 02:41:05,751
[SPEAKER_01]: And they can move around too.

1901
02:41:05,811 --> 02:41:11,717
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the CD board has been bopping between Tuesdays or Wednesdays and Thursdays.

1902
02:41:11,897 --> 02:41:16,021
[SPEAKER_01]: They recently had one Monday, that's when they could make it work.

1903
02:41:17,622 --> 02:41:21,706
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think the day of the night is codified anywhere about this either.

1904
02:41:22,326 --> 02:41:24,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It was more about what's available.

1905
02:41:24,348 --> 02:41:29,313
[SPEAKER_01]: I think a little bit is that Dennis is staffing a bunch of boards, but I would say that

1906
02:41:29,980 --> 02:41:44,628
[SPEAKER_01]: OSBT-Karen Hollweg, OSBT-Michael Leccese, he's a paid clerk for this board and for some of the others he mostly just manages their zoom and stuff and we could arrange to have a different planner in the office staff a meeting if he needs to be at this meeting.

1907
02:41:45,568 --> 02:41:50,151
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, um I have a recollection I don't know if it's specifically codified but.

1908
02:41:50,664 --> 02:41:56,427
[SPEAKER_04]: It was somewhere that I think these meetings are supposed to be on Mondays or like a couple of years ago, they were on Mondays.

1909
02:41:56,868 --> 02:42:05,232
[SPEAKER_05]: And then we started on Tuesdays and then city council asked us to move because we were conflicting with them, even though we were in the same building.

1910
02:42:05,252 --> 02:42:09,975
[SPEAKER_05]: And it actually was good for the council because the council would come and speak at our meetings, but they asked us to move.

1911
02:42:09,995 --> 02:42:13,017
[SPEAKER_05]: So we moved to Mondays and then we did Mondays for about a year.

1912
02:42:13,157 --> 02:42:18,900
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, and then the school committee asked us, could we move from Mondays because we were conflicting with them and.

1913
02:42:19,770 --> 02:42:23,673
[SPEAKER_05]: I staffed the concom, which meets on Wednesdays, so that pretty much led us to having meetings on Thursdays.

1914
02:42:24,234 --> 02:42:24,814
[SPEAKER_01]: And here we are.

1915
02:42:25,835 --> 02:42:26,095
[SPEAKER_05]: Right.

1916
02:42:26,215 --> 02:42:27,476
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess that's a good point.

1917
02:42:27,576 --> 02:42:39,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It's interesting if the school committee and the city council care that you're at the same time, right?

1918
02:42:39,325 --> 02:42:44,229
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been thinking more logistics, like possible, like what can we make it work?

1919
02:42:47,405 --> 02:42:49,046
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry, my job is whining.

1920
02:42:51,267 --> 02:42:54,788
[SPEAKER_01]: We did when we did the rezoning.

1921
02:42:55,508 --> 02:43:04,932
[SPEAKER_01]: One of the things we were very, I was very careful about is that we did not put anything as specific as number of times or dates or days of the week.

1922
02:43:06,790 --> 02:43:13,556
[SPEAKER_01]: into zoning, because I feel very strongly that that's not something that should be in an ordinance, it should be in a rule or regulation.

1923
02:43:13,576 --> 02:43:17,620
[SPEAKER_01]: It should be votable by the board and not by law.

1924
02:43:19,642 --> 02:43:25,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the city council's meetings are in their rules and regulations and not in the ordinances.

1925
02:43:27,829 --> 02:43:29,610
[SPEAKER_19]: Yeah, it's a problem.

1926
02:43:31,646 --> 02:43:31,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1927
02:43:32,606 --> 02:43:33,526
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, something to think about.

1928
02:43:33,647 --> 02:43:35,787
[SPEAKER_04]: I definitely would be up for it being any other night.

1929
02:43:35,827 --> 02:43:37,387
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm usually traveling on Thursday nights.

1930
02:43:38,968 --> 02:43:39,768
[SPEAKER_04]: But anyway.

1931
02:43:39,788 --> 02:43:42,269
[SPEAKER_04]: I just want to think about that.

1932
02:43:42,809 --> 02:43:45,009
[SPEAKER_19]: Check in meeting minutes.

1933
02:43:45,129 --> 02:43:52,551
[SPEAKER_19]: I know there's a lot going on, but is there a strategy to get caught up on those?

1934
02:43:52,611 --> 02:43:54,191
[SPEAKER_19]: Yeah.

1935
02:43:54,291 --> 02:43:58,272
[SPEAKER_05]: I've got a few sort of in the works, so I'll get them to you, and we'll have them for the next meeting.

1936
02:43:58,793 --> 02:44:00,773
[SPEAKER_05]: I think I had a few that I may just not have sent to you.

1937
02:44:01,361 --> 02:44:02,682
[SPEAKER_05]: So I will get them to you.

1938
02:44:03,342 --> 02:44:03,842
[SPEAKER_19]: OK, thanks.

1939
02:44:03,902 --> 02:44:07,364
[SPEAKER_19]: Yeah, I think the last one I recall approving might be fed.

1940
02:44:07,644 --> 02:44:08,544
[SPEAKER_19]: It was Jan or fed.

1941
02:44:08,725 --> 02:44:12,186
[SPEAKER_05]: So I definitely know I have March and April.

1942
02:44:12,226 --> 02:44:16,348
[SPEAKER_04]: So similar and different.

1943
02:44:16,368 --> 02:44:18,549
[SPEAKER_04]: I have been writing all the opinions.

1944
02:44:19,009 --> 02:44:24,112
[SPEAKER_04]: I think that's just because the last chair and I used to write all of them together or split them.

1945
02:44:24,552 --> 02:44:26,413
[SPEAKER_04]: And I'm getting a bit of a backlog.

1946
02:44:26,513 --> 02:44:29,975
[SPEAKER_04]: So if folks are OK with this, I was hoping that going forward,

1947
02:44:31,533 --> 02:44:47,638
[SPEAKER_04]: I could assign them on a rotating basis and then people, a different member, voting member for each case could be in charge of drafting up the opinions and then send to me and I can do the final review before we sign them if folks are cool with that.

1948
02:44:52,500 --> 02:44:53,560
[SPEAKER_07]: Good practice.

1949
02:44:56,151 --> 02:44:57,992
[SPEAKER_04]: OK, great, so not hearing any no's.

1950
02:44:58,092 --> 02:45:01,454
[SPEAKER_19]: Well, I just, you know, I want to be a team player.

1951
02:45:01,534 --> 02:45:05,496
[SPEAKER_19]: I think maybe at a minimum, we'd want everybody trained beforehand.

1952
02:45:05,516 --> 02:45:11,640
[SPEAKER_19]: I just, I know that the opinions are very kind of baked in the law of it all.

1953
02:45:11,680 --> 02:45:17,743
[SPEAKER_19]: And so just because you're the lawyer doesn't mean you get to forever do all the opinions.

1954
02:45:17,784 --> 02:45:22,346
[SPEAKER_19]: But I do think that you probably should at least give people a primer as to.

1955
02:45:23,087 --> 02:45:24,427
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and you know, I'm happy.

1956
02:45:25,740 --> 02:45:26,420
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's a good point.

1957
02:45:27,040 --> 02:45:32,102
[SPEAKER_04]: I'd be happy to, particularly the first couple of times people write an opinion, work with them on it.

1958
02:45:32,522 --> 02:45:36,403
[SPEAKER_04]: I also have a template that I use for certain things, so we can definitely go over all of that.

1959
02:45:37,363 --> 02:45:46,925
[SPEAKER_04]: But just especially with what I'm expecting is a busy fall, it would be helpful to me if I didn't have to write all of them every month.

1960
02:45:47,666 --> 02:45:51,347
[SPEAKER_04]: Because then it's constantly emailing me and being like, do you have decisions yet?

1961
02:45:51,407 --> 02:45:53,027
[SPEAKER_04]: And I'm like, I'm working on it.

1962
02:45:54,784 --> 02:45:58,425
[SPEAKER_04]: Something we can all talk about and figure out as we go forward, and I'm happy to work with people.

1963
02:45:58,565 --> 02:45:59,166
[SPEAKER_04]: Alicia, what was that?

1964
02:45:59,186 --> 02:46:05,688
[SPEAKER_01]: I just want to throw this out there, Jacqueline, that I'm pretty sure we could have KP law help with decisions as well.

1965
02:46:06,548 --> 02:46:10,209
[SPEAKER_04]: I'd rather not do that if it's all the same.

1966
02:46:11,369 --> 02:46:14,790
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't think I would feel comfortable with that, but I do appreciate that.

1967
02:46:15,591 --> 02:46:15,891
[SPEAKER_01]: OK.

1968
02:46:16,111 --> 02:46:19,372
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that other boards use legal counsel to write the decisions.

1969
02:46:20,557 --> 02:46:32,005
[SPEAKER_01]: So in fact, our former chair, Alan, not Alan Arena, Alan Arena's father, Mr. Arena, used to write decisions for other boards as part of his legal practice.

1970
02:46:32,305 --> 02:46:33,886
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why I'm putting that out there.

1971
02:46:34,206 --> 02:46:35,047
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

1972
02:46:38,690 --> 02:46:46,475
[SPEAKER_04]: I would prefer not to use KP Law, just personal preference, but anyway, something we can all think about.

1973
02:46:49,470 --> 02:46:53,631
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, should we vote to adjourn?

1974
02:46:54,251 --> 02:46:55,211
[SPEAKER_19]: Motion to adjourn.

1975
02:46:56,312 --> 02:46:58,412
[SPEAKER_19]: Second.

1976
02:46:58,492 --> 02:47:01,373
[SPEAKER_04]: I'll do roll call because it said we need to.

1977
02:47:01,453 --> 02:47:02,213
[SPEAKER_04]: Jim, vote.

1978
02:47:06,394 --> 02:47:06,614
[SPEAKER_14]: Yes.

1979
02:47:07,274 --> 02:47:07,495
[SPEAKER_04]: Mike.

1980
02:47:08,095 --> 02:47:08,355
[SPEAKER_14]: Yes.

1981
02:47:08,955 --> 02:47:09,255
[SPEAKER_04]: Yvette.

1982
02:47:09,875 --> 02:47:10,095
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

1983
02:47:10,715 --> 02:47:10,955
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

1984
02:47:11,676 --> 02:47:12,756
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, and I'm a yes.

1985
02:47:12,796 --> 02:47:14,216
[SPEAKER_04]: Thanks folks, we'll see you soon.

1986
02:47:14,676 --> 02:47:15,336
[SPEAKER_10]: Thanks so much.

1987
02:47:15,356 --> 02:47:17,317
[SPEAKER_10]: Have a great fourth.

1988
02:47:18,159 --> 02:47:19,401
[SPEAKER_04]: Have a great fall.

1989
02:47:19,762 --> 02:47:20,243
[SPEAKER_10]: Be safe.

1990
02:47:21,565 --> 02:47:22,267
[SPEAKER_10]: Nice meeting everybody.

